// monitor-agreement.test.maxon — the two DebugStream ring CONSUMERS read one traced binary and agree.
//
// A `--debugstream` build emits a producer that writes fixed-size records into a ring in a named shared
// section. `maxon monitor --filter=mm <exe>` is the other half: it creates the section, spawns the binary
// into it, decodes every `mm` event and prints it, and exits with the CHILD's own code. shv2's spec runner
// depends on that consumer for every `<!-- MmTrace -->` case it runs, so shv2 needs one of its own — and
// the only way to know a second decoder reads the same wire format is to run BOTH over the SAME bytes.
//
// ⚠ **THE `bin/maxon` ARM IS A TRANSITION ORACLE AND RETIRES WITH THE BOOTSTRAP.** What this file gates is
// AGREEMENT, which needs two decoders; once the bootstrap is retired there is one, and this case has no
// subject left. Until then the bootstrap's decoder is the reference the new one is measured against, in
// exactly the sense `tests/README.md` rule 6 means: a hand-written expectation would prove only that shv2
// does what its author wrote down.
//
// ⛔ **THE COMPARISON IS BETWEEN THE TWO MONITORS, NEVER AGAINST A STORED TRANSCRIPT.** What the fixture
// allocates is the memory manager's business and moves whenever `StringBuilder` or `Array` does; a golden
// list of events here would go red for changes that have nothing to do with either decoder. The events are
// normalized only where they carry a property of the RUN rather than of the program — the `[+SSSS.mmm]`
// timestamp — and compared to each other.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A MONITOR'S STDOUT IS TWO HALVES AND BOTH ARE COMPARED.** The decoded events are one; the child's
// OWN output, which a monitor forwards verbatim, is the other, and a decoder comparison is blind to it. The
// fixture prints a BLANK line for that half's sake: it is the one line a forwarder that reads the empty
// string as "the pipe had nothing" drops, silently, with every event still identical.
//
// ⛔ **TWO EMPTY LISTS MUST NOT READ AS AGREEMENT.** A monitor that attached to nothing, or decoded a
// family it does not have, answers zero events, and so does its twin; the equality alone would be green.
// The count is therefore asserted non-zero on BOTH sides before the lists are compared, and the exit code
// is asserted first of all — a monitor that never ran the child cannot have read the child's ring.
//
// ⛔ **EVERYTHING THAT WOULD STOP THE CASE RUNNING AT ALL PANICS RATHER THAN FAILING AN ASSERTION**: a
// spawn that never happened, a build that did not produce the program, a stamp in a shape neither monitor
// writes. None of those is a decoder answering wrongly, and a report that made them read alike would send
// a reader looking for a defect in the thing that never ran.
//
// Staging, spawning and the folds applied to a captured stream come from
// `byte-identical-debug-info.test.maxon`, this directory's shared half; only what this case alone uses is
// declared here.

// The capture command, spelled as `SpecTestRunner.maxon` spells it.
let MonitorSubcommand = "monitor"
let MonitorMmFilterArg = "--filter=mm"

// The flag that puts the producer in the binary. Without it the binary emits none at all and every monitor
// reads an empty ring — the one failure this file's non-zero count assertions exist to catch.
let DebugStreamFlag = "--debugstream"
let TracedBuildLabel = "traced"

// The prefix a monitor puts on every line it AUTHORS, `[+SSSS.mmm] `, and the payload prefix of a decoded
// `mm` event. The timestamp is a property of the run and is stripped. The stamp is also what splits the two
// halves of a monitor's stdout: a line carrying it is the monitor's, and the complement is the child's own
// output forwarded into the same stream.
let MonitorLinePrefix = "[+"
let MonitorTimestampEnd = "] "
let MmTraceEventPrefix = "mm_"

// ⭐ **HOW FAR APART THE TWO MONITORS' FIRST EVENTS MAY BE STAMPED.** `[+SSSS.mmm]` is elapsed time since
// THAT monitor attached, so both first events land a few tens of milliseconds into their own run whenever
// each monitor seeded the segment header with the counter the producer subtracts from. The two runs are
// sequential and each builds nothing, so the whole spread available to an honest pair is a spawn.
//
// ⛔ **RELATIVE TO EACH OTHER, NEVER AN ABSOLUTE CEILING.** A monitor that seeds the wrong counter reports
// SYSTEM UPTIME, which on a freshly booted host is small — so a bound like "under a minute" would pass
// there for exactly the wrong reason. Comparing the two monitors has no such blind spot.
let AllowedTimestampSkewMs = 5000

// The `[+SSSS.mmm]` stamp's own spelling: what separates its two halves, and how many digits a millisecond
// is written in.
let TimestampFractionPoint = "."
let TimestampStampHalves = 2
let TimestampFractionDigits = 3
let MillisecondsPerSecond = 1000

// What the fixture returns, and therefore what a monitor must return: the monitor's exit code is the
// CHILD's. `main` builds the 4-byte string "abcd" and answers its byte length.
let FixtureExitCode = 4

// Milliseconds a monitor reports since it attached. Deliberately WIDE: a monitor whose seed is not the
// counter the producer subtracts from reports system uptime, and that reading is what this file exists to
// catch rather than to make unrepresentable.
typealias ElapsedMs = int(0 to u64.max)

// ============================================================================================
// THE CONTRACT
// ============================================================================================

test 'both monitors read one traced binary the same way'
	let staged = stagedProgram("monitor-agreement", fixture: TraceFixtureName)
	buildTracedProgram(staged)

	let referenceRun = runMonitor(BootstrapCompilerStem, staged: staged)
	let referenceExit = referenceRun.exitCode()
	try Expect.equal(referenceExit as AssertedInt, expected: FixtureExitCode, message: "the reference monitor `{BootstrapCompilerStem} {MonitorSubcommand}` did not answer the traced child's own exit code. A monitor that did not run the child cannot have read its ring, so nothing below this line would mean anything. It wrote to stderr: {referenceRun.stderr}")

	let referenceEvents = decodedEvents(referenceRun)
	try Expect.notEqual(referenceEvents.count() as AssertedInt, expected: 0, message: "the reference monitor decoded ZERO `{MmTraceEventPrefix}` events from a binary built with `{DebugStreamFlag}`. This is the positive control: without it two decoders that both read nothing would compare equal and this case would be green having measured nothing. It printed: {referenceRun.stdout}")

	let testedRun = runMonitor(TestedCompilerStem, staged: staged)
	let testedExit = testedRun.exitCode()
	try Expect.equal(testedExit as AssertedInt, expected: FixtureExitCode, message: "`{TestedCompilerStem} {MonitorSubcommand} {MonitorMmFilterArg}` did not answer the traced child's own exit code. It printed: {testedRun.stdout} — and wrote to stderr: {testedRun.stderr}")

	let testedEvents = decodedEvents(testedRun)
	try Expect.notEqual(testedEvents.count() as AssertedInt, expected: 0, message: "`{TestedCompilerStem} {MonitorSubcommand}` decoded ZERO `{MmTraceEventPrefix}` events from the same binary the reference monitor read {referenceEvents.count()} out of. It printed: {testedRun.stdout}")

	let tested = renderedLines(testedEvents)
	let reference = renderedLines(referenceEvents)
	try Expect.equal(tested, expected: reference, message: "the two monitors decoded the SAME ring differently. Both read one binary, one run each, with only the `{MonitorLinePrefix}SSSS.mmm{MonitorTimestampEnd}` timestamp stripped — so a difference here is one decoder disagreeing with the other about the wire format the producer writes, not a change in what the fixture allocates.")

	// ⭐ **AND THE OTHER HALF OF A MONITOR'S STDOUT: THE CHILD'S OWN OUTPUT, FORWARDED.** A decoder comparison
	// is blind to it — both event lists stay identical while a forwarder edits what the program printed — and
	// the fixture's BLANK line is exactly the line a forwarder keyed off an empty string drops.
	let referenceForwarded = forwardedChildLines(referenceRun)
	try Expect.notEqual(referenceForwarded.count() as AssertedInt, expected: 0, message: "the reference monitor forwarded NONE of the child's own stdout, though the fixture prints three lines. This is the positive control for the comparison below: two monitors that both forwarded nothing compare equal. It printed: {referenceRun.stdout}")

	let testedForwarded = renderedLines(forwardedChildLines(testedRun))
	let expectedForwarded = renderedLines(referenceForwarded)
	try Expect.equal(testedForwarded, expected: expectedForwarded, message: "the two monitors forwarded the child's OWN stdout differently. One binary, one run each, and a monitor relays what its child printed verbatim — so a difference here is one of them EDITING that output. A blank line is the one a reader that reads the empty string as `nothing was buffered` drops silently.")

	// ⭐ **AND THE HALF THE COMPARISON ABOVE STRIPS.** The payloads agree whichever counter a monitor seeded
	// the header with, because the producer writes them the same either way — so the stamp is the only place
	// a disagreement about the CLOCK can show, and the equality above is blind to it by construction.
	let referenceStampMs = firstEventTimestampMs(referenceRun, monitorLabel: BootstrapCompilerStem)
	let testedStampMs = firstEventTimestampMs(testedRun, monitorLabel: TestedCompilerStem)
	let skewMs = testedStampMs - referenceStampMs if testedStampMs > referenceStampMs else referenceStampMs - testedStampMs
	try Expect.atMost(skewMs as AssertedInt, than: AllowedTimestampSkewMs, message: "the two monitors stamped their first `{MmTraceEventPrefix}` event {skewMs} ms apart — {BootstrapCompilerStem} said {referenceStampMs} ms and {TestedCompilerStem} said {testedStampMs} ms. `{MonitorLinePrefix}SSSS.mmm{MonitorTimestampEnd}` is elapsed time since THAT monitor attached, so a gap this size means one of them seeded the segment header's start timestamp with a counter the producer does not subtract from, and every line it printed carries that counter itself rather than an elapsed time.")
end 'both monitors read one traced binary the same way'

// ============================================================================================
// SPAWNING
// ============================================================================================

// Compile the staged program with the DebugStream producer in it. A build that did not succeed is the gate
// being unable to run rather than a decoder answering wrongly, so it panics carrying the compiler's words.
function buildTracedProgram(staged FilePath)
	var extraArguments = StringArray.create()
	extraArguments.push(DebugStreamFlag)

	let run = spawnBuild(staged, label: TracedBuildLabel, extraArguments: extraArguments)
	if run.exitCode() != 0 'didNotBuild'
		panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — `{TestedCompilerStem} {BuildCommandName} … {DebugStreamFlag}` exited {run.exitCode()}, so there is no traced binary for either monitor to read. It wrote to stderr: {run.stderr}")
	end 'didNotBuild'

	if not File.exists(builtExecutable(staged)) 'wroteNothing'
		panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — the traced build exited 0 and wrote no executable to {builtExecutable(staged).toString()}.")
	end 'wroteNothing'
end 'buildTracedProgram'

// Run the traced binary under one monitor and collect everything it said.
function runMonitor(binaryStem String, staged FilePath) returns CollectedOutput
	var arguments = StringArray.create()
	arguments.push(MonitorSubcommand)
	arguments.push(MonitorMmFilterArg)
	arguments.push(builtExecutable(staged).toString())

	return spawnCompiler(arguments, staged: staged, what: "the '{binaryStem}' monitor", binaryStem: binaryStem)
end 'runMonitor'

// ============================================================================================
// READING A MONITOR'S OUTPUT
// ============================================================================================

// The `mm` events one monitor decoded, in the order it printed them, each stripped of the timestamp it was
// authored with. Anything that is not an event of the family — the child's own forwarded stdout, a blank
// line, a summary — is dropped by the same test, which is what makes this fold idempotent.
function decodedEvents(run CollectedOutput) returns StringArray
	var events = StringArray.create()

	for line in outputLines(run.stdout) 'eachLine'
		let payload = withoutMonitorTimestamp(line)
		if payload.startsWith(MmTraceEventPrefix) 'anEvent'
			events.push(payload)
		end 'anEvent'
	end 'eachLine'

	return events
end 'decodedEvents'

// One line without the `[+SSSS.mmm] ` a monitor authored it with. The elapsed time is a property of the RUN,
// so two monitors can only be compared past it. Only the FIRST marker goes: a `] ` inside an event's own
// payload is part of the event.
function withoutMonitorTimestamp(line String) returns String
	if not line.startsWith(MonitorLinePrefix) 'notAuthoredByAMonitor'
		return line
	end 'notAuthoredByAMonitor'

	var cursor = try line.findFirst(MonitorTimestampEnd) otherwise return line
	for _ in 0 upto MonitorTimestampEnd.count() 'eachMarkerGrapheme'
		cursor = try line.indexAfter(cursor) otherwise return line
	end 'eachMarkerGrapheme'

	return line.slice(cursor, endIndex: line.endIndex())
end 'withoutMonitorTimestamp'

// ⭐ **THE FIRST `mm` EVENT'S OWN STAMP, IN MILLISECONDS.** The lists compared in the contract are stripped
// of it because it is a property of the RUN; this reads exactly what that stripping discards.
function firstEventTimestampMs(run CollectedOutput, monitorLabel String) returns ElapsedMs
	for line in outputLines(run.stdout) 'eachLine'
		if line.startsWith(MonitorLinePrefix) and withoutMonitorTimestamp(line).startsWith(MmTraceEventPrefix) 'anEvent'
			return millisecondsIn(monitorTimestampText(line, monitorLabel: monitorLabel), monitorLabel: monitorLabel)
		end 'anEvent'
	end 'eachLine'

	panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — '{monitorLabel}' printed no `{MmTraceEventPrefix}` line carrying a `{MonitorLinePrefix}SSSS.mmm{MonitorTimestampEnd}` stamp, though the count asserted above says it decoded events. It printed: {run.stdout}")
end 'firstEventTimestampMs'

// The digits between `[+` and `] `. Both markers were established by the caller, so an absence here is the
// instrument broken rather than a decoder answering wrongly.
function monitorTimestampText(line String, monitorLabel String) returns String
	var cursor = line.startIndex()
	for _ in 0 upto MonitorLinePrefix.count() 'eachPrefixGrapheme'
		cursor = try line.indexAfter(cursor) otherwise panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — '{monitorLabel}' authored a line shorter than the `{MonitorLinePrefix}` it starts with: {line}")
	end 'eachPrefixGrapheme'

	let markerAt = try line.findFirst(MonitorTimestampEnd) otherwise panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — '{monitorLabel}' authored a `{MonitorLinePrefix}` line with no `{MonitorTimestampEnd}` marker: {line}")

	return line.slice(cursor, endIndex: markerAt)
end 'monitorTimestampText'

// `SSSS.mmm` as one whole number of milliseconds. A stamp that is not that shape is a monitor that changed
// its output format, which no assertion below would report intelligibly — so the spelling is checked here.
function millisecondsIn(stamp String, monitorLabel String) returns ElapsedMs
	let halves = stamp.split(TimestampFractionPoint)
	if halves.count() != TimestampStampHalves 'notSecondsAndMillis'
		panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — '{monitorLabel}' stamped an event `{stamp}`, which is not the `SSSS{TimestampFractionPoint}mmm` shape both monitors write.")
	end 'notSecondsAndMillis'

	let seconds = try halves.get(0) otherwise panic("monitor-agreement.test: a two-element split has a first element")
	let millis = try halves.get(1) otherwise panic("monitor-agreement.test: a two-element split has a second element")
	if millis.count() != TimestampFractionDigits 'notMilliseconds'
		panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — '{monitorLabel}' stamped an event `{stamp}`, whose fraction is {millis.count()} digits rather than the {TimestampFractionDigits} a millisecond is written in.")
	end 'notMilliseconds'

	return decimalValue(seconds, stamp: stamp, monitorLabel: monitorLabel) * MillisecondsPerSecond + decimalValue(millis, stamp: stamp, monitorLabel: monitorLabel)
end 'millisecondsIn'

// One half of the stamp as a number.
function decimalValue(digits String, stamp String, monitorLabel String) returns ElapsedMs
	if digits.isEmpty() 'noDigits'
		panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — '{monitorLabel}' stamped an event `{stamp}` with an empty half.")
	end 'noDigits'

	var value = 0
	for c in digits 'eachCharacter'
		if not Ascii.isDigit(c) 'notADigit'
			panic("monitor-agreement.test: the gate could not run — '{monitorLabel}' stamped an event `{stamp}`, which is not written in digits.")
		end 'notADigit'

		value = value * DecimalBase + hexDigitValue(c)
	end 'eachCharacter'

	return value
end 'decimalValue'

// One list of lines as the text a failure report shows, so a disagreement is read as a diff rather than as
// two array identities. Shared by both comparisons — the decoded events and the child's forwarded output —
// because a list of lines renders the same way whoever wrote them.
//
// ⛔ **NOT the shared half's `renderedRows`, which INDENTS.** This text is what the equality assertions
// COMPARE, not merely what they report, and a leading indent would make the fixture's blank line — the one
// a broken forwarder drops — indistinguishable from a line carrying two spaces.
function renderedLines(lines StringArray) returns String
	var rendered = ""
	for line in lines 'eachLine'
		rendered.append("{line}{LineFeed}")
	end 'eachLine'

	return rendered
end 'renderedLines'

// ⭐ **THE CHILD'S OWN STDOUT, WHICH A MONITOR FORWARDS VERBATIM RATHER THAN DECODES.** Every line a monitor
// AUTHORED carries the `[+SSSS.mmm] ` stamp, so the complement is the child's — the half `decodedEvents`
// drops.
//
// ⛔ **A BLANK LINE IS ONE OF THEM AND IT IS THE ONE FORWARDING GETS WRONG**: a reader that treats the empty
// string as "the pipe had nothing" swallows it, and every event list stays identical while it does. That is
// why the list is compared WHOLE rather than filtered to non-empty lines.
function forwardedChildLines(run CollectedOutput) returns StringArray
	var forwarded = StringArray.create()

	for line in outputLines(run.stdout) 'eachLine'
		if not line.startsWith(MonitorLinePrefix) 'theChildsOwn'
			forwarded.push(line)
		end 'theChildsOwn'
	end 'eachLine'

	// `split` leaves an empty element after a final newline, and every monitor's last write ends in one — so
	// that element belongs to the split rather than to anything either of them printed.
	if endsWithEmptyLine(forwarded) 'trailingNewline'
		_ = try forwarded.pop() otherwise panic("monitor-agreement.test: a list that ends with an empty line is not empty")
	end 'trailingNewline'

	return forwarded
end 'forwardedChildLines'

function endsWithEmptyLine(lines StringArray) returns bool
	if lines.isEmpty() 'nothingThere'
		return false
	end 'nothingThere'

	let last = try lines.get(lines.count() - 1) otherwise panic("monitor-agreement.test: a non-empty list has a last element")

	return last.isEmpty()
end 'endsWithEmptyLine'
